[FINAL-TEXT] The final evidence is decisive. The dmesg shows at [72282-72283] that **all five bridges** — br_v1, br_v5, br_v6, br_v100, br_v200 — go disabled within less than one second of each other. This is the unmistakable signature of the entire sshswitch container being stopped (Kathara lab teardown). The same all-bridges-down pattern appears at every other "flap" epoch. And the 20-packet ping from PC1 completes with **zero packet loss** and steady ~0.6ms RTT.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — that the VLAN5 default gateway (10.5.0.254) "drops out for a few seconds at random" — is a false premise. No gateway fault exists in the current lab.

The true gateway for the 10.5.0.0/24 segment is the Linux bridge `br_v5` on **sshswitch**, which holds the SVI address 10.5.0.254/24. The forwarding path is PC1/PC2 → switch4 (VLAN5 access) → trunk to sshswitch.eth0 → eth0.5 subinterface → br_v5. At investigation time, br_v5 is UP with no interface errors, the bond0 uplink to router17 reports zero link failures on both slaves, and eth0.5 is clean. Direct pings from both PC1 (10.5.0.1) and PC2 (10.5.0.2) to 10.5.0.254 complete with 0% packet loss and sub-millisecond latency across 20 consecutive probes.

The kernel dmesg does contain entries showing br_v5 ports entering "disabled state," which at first glance resembles an interface-flap log. However, close inspection reveals that at every such epoch, ALL five bridges on sshswitch (br_v1, br_v5, br_v6, br_v100, br_v200) go down within under one second of each other, and tap device removal messages (e.g., `port 1(tapa5712076) entered disabled state`) appear in the same window. This is the definitive kernel signature of Kathara stopping the sshswitch container — a lab restart cycle — not an in-session interface flap. The "downtime" durations of roughly 28-32 seconds correspond to the container teardown-and-restart interval.

Root cause: the user's premise is incorrect — no LAN interface on the gateway is flapping.
Corrective action: none required; the gateway is operating normally.
=== END ===